  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Blustery and cold conditions expected today and \ntonight with mountain flurries and snow showers. Snow may \naccumulate 1-3 inches at elevations above 3500 feet.\n\nGonna feel a little winter-like across the mountains today as \nthe parent upper level low associated with yesterdays coastal \nstorm passes overhead. Cold air aloft with 850 mb temps near M6 \nC and winds of 35kts will promote blustery and cold conditions \nnear ground level with our mountain counties catching the brunt \nof the winter interlude. Flurries and periods of light snow are \nexpected west of the Blue Ridge with the western slopes near the\nAppalachian Divide picking up some accumulating snow. \nElevations above 3500 feet are expected to remain below freezing\nthrough the day, so anything that falls should stick. Web Cam \nfrom Beech Mtn NC suggests about an inch of snow has fallen. \nUntreated roads are snow covered at this elevation...air \ntemperature in the mid 20s. It will take most of the day for \nthe upper low to cross the mountains, so anticipate adding \nanother inch or two on top of what is already there. Cloud bases\nwill tend to hug the ridge-tops too, so expect rime ice from \nthe super-cooled cloud droplets in addition to the snow.\n\nFor elevations below 3500 feet, air temperatures should climb \nabove freezing today, so opportunity for snow to accumulate will\nnot be as forthcoming. If there is a more intense period of \nsnow, then maybe yes, could see a dusting. \n\nAs for areas east of the Blue Ridge, the westerly downslope \nwind flow will mute precip production. Temperatures are also \nexpected to rise back close to 50 degrees for the piedmont...a \ncool day but not as winter-like. It will, however, remain\nblustery with wind gusts of 20-30 mph. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Temperatures recover to near normal this \nweekend. Next chance of rain is Sunday night. \n\nAfter a cold night tonight, temperatures moderate for Saturday\nand Sunday returning to near normal. Flow aloft becomes zonal \nfor Saturday, cold air aloft exiting the region with normal \ndiurnal/nocturnal temperature profile becoming re-established. \n\nLooking at highs Saturday ranging from the 40s/50s mountains to \nnear 60 in the piedmont.\n\nA cold front will approach the area Sunday and pass overhead\nSunday night. Southwest winds will promote warm air advection\nahead front with increasing moisture resulting in increasing\ncloudiness. Rain then moves into the western mountains during\nthe late afternoon and passing over the remainder of the CWA\nSunday night. Current projections are for a quarter inch or less\nof rain...the front remaining progressive as it crosses the\nforecast area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Dry weather expected Monday through Thursday of \nnext week.\n\nDry weather returns Monday with near seasonal temperatures\nanticipated for most of next week. Zonal flow aloft is forecast\nfor much of the country early in the week. El Nino will enhance\nanother southern stream storm with impacts to California \nTuesday-Wednesday, the energy from this feature progressing east\nand reaching our part of the country by the weekend. \n\nThe CPC Outlook leans into the southern stream remaining active\nwith the 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks reflecting above normal \nchances for precipitation from coast to coast but skewed to the \nsouthern CONUS. Not a lot of cold air to be found, so the most \nof the precipitation associated with these systems should be \nrain.",
